// Card leveling (0→10) (2026-09-18, Epic B). Stat curve: ACCELERATING, per the roadmap's own
// framing ("mobile card games usually accelerate...to make level 10 feel like a real
// milestone") — +6% per level plus a small quadratic kicker, landing at exactly +100% (double
// stats) at level 10: multiplier(L) = 1 + 0.06*L + 0.004*L². Applies to attack/health only,
// never cost/wait — baked into getCardDefs() below (which also floors each boosted stat at
// base+level so a low-stat card's early levels are never rounded away to nothing), the one
// existing choke point already used by the Codex/deck-editor/engine, rather than a separate
// lookup every call site would need to remember. Known limitation (documented, not silently
// papered over): stats are still looked up
// per-defId in one shared table, so a leveled card's boost isn't scoped away from an AI
// opponent who happens to field the exact same card (DEFAULT_DECK's fixed 8-card roster) — a
// real design question for the next pass, not something this session's engine architecture
// supports cleanly without deeper per-owner stat plumbing.
// Rebalanced 2026-09-23 (batch #27) — the original curve below is kept ONLY as a documented
// historical record of a real, data-confirmed balance bug, not as dead code to resurrect:
//   OLD: return 1 + 0.06*L + 0.004*L*L;  (2.0x stats at level 10)
//   OLD floor in getCardDefs(): Math.max(1, base+lvl, Math.round(base*mult))  (+1 flat per level)
// A engine-level simulation (bramblewood-engine.js's own simulateOneMatch, 300-400 games per
// matchup, mirror decks differing only by level) found the OLD curve was catastrophically broken:
// a level 0 vs level 1 mirror match — a SINGLE level-up, nominally a 6.4% stat multiplier — won
// ~98% of games for the leveled side, because the flat "+1 per level" floor (added to guarantee a
// 1-attack card's level-up is visible) actually dominated the percentage curve for the median card
// on the real 244-card roster (median attack is 4, health 12 — a flat +1/level floor overtakes a
// 6.4%-a-level percentage curve immediately, not just for the low-stat edge case the comment
// assumed it was scoped to). The code's own prior comment claimed the percentage curve "dominates
// once base stats are big enough" — empirically false for the actual roster.
// The deeper finding, unaffected by the floor bug: this combat engine's attrition dynamics
// compound a uniform deck-wide stat boost hard — even a conservative, floor-free 30%-at-max-level
// curve alone still won ~100% of mirror games by level 5+ in the same simulation. That's a
// structural property of a system where more health survives more exchanges and more attack ends
// them faster, both compounding per unit across a whole deck, not something a gentler percentage
// alone fixes. Since there is currently no live player-vs-player deck matchmaking (Online Raid is
// solo-vs-fixed-boss; the "Async Arena" PvP concept is still unbuilt per the roadmap), a real power
// swing across levels is presently a PvE progression-power-fantasy question, not a competitive-
// fairness one — but flagging clearly for Jay: if real deck-vs-deck PvP ever ships, a fully-leveled
// deck will still be a heavy, near-deterministic favorite over an unleveled one under ANY curve
// this shape, and that would need either level-aware matchmaking, a ranked-mode level cap, or an
// intentionally much flatter curve at that point — not something this fix alone resolves.
// A second sim pass tried keeping a SMALL additive floor (capped +2, phased in from level 5) on
// top of a gentler 30%-at-max curve — it still won 99-100% of mirror games by level 8, barely
// better than the original bug. A third pass, an even smaller +1-at-level-8+ floor on top of a
// 15%-at-max curve, STILL jumped straight to 98-99% at level 8-10 (vs. 87.7% for the same curve
// with no floor at all). That's the real, load-bearing finding: this engine's per-unit attrition
// compounding is sensitive enough that ANY uniform additive bonus across a whole deck — even +1,
// even only in the last 2-3 levels — reliably tips a mirror match to near-deterministic, no matter
// how gentle the percentage curve underneath it is. So this fix drops the additive floor entirely
// rather than trying to shrink it further; a percentage-only curve is the only shape that stayed
// in a sane range (a real edge, not a coin-flip-breaker) all the way up to full investment.
// Final curve, chosen from the sim sweep: 15% total stat increase at level 10 produced 87.7% win
// rate for a fully-maxed mirror deck (a strong, worthwhile-feeling investment, not a guaranteed
// win) and 62.7% at level 5 (a real but modest edge partway through) — the best balance found
// between "leveling matters" and "leveling doesn't break the game," given how hard this specific
// combat system amplifies any deck-wide edge.
// Trade-off accepted, and flagged rather than silently absorbed: because there's no additive
// floor anymore, a very low base stat (attack 1-2) may show literally no numeric change on some
